-- sw_driver_timing.lua.template -- Mesen (cycle-accurate) measurement of phase 2 slice 4b's own
-- per-frame movement driver (sw_update_player, reached from engine/player.asm's update_player),
-- discharging design-streamed-worlds.md's obligation 1 ("Driver specification and timing... The
-- 660-cycle / 2.39% margin is PROVISIONAL until [the production driver is measured in the real
-- engine on UNROM 512 and the frame bound recomputed from it]") AND fix round 2's own Finding D/
-- ruling M, which found the obligation-1 discharge incomplete: the old fixture (a 12x2 grid, landing
-- (5,0)) never reached the real 61-region-tall packing ceiling UNROM 512 actually allows, carried no
-- real entity workload, never exercised the row axis or an axis handoff, and had no ownership/
-- respawn evidence beyond sw_col changing (a crossing that moved sw_col but never actually spawned
-- the new screen's entities would have passed unnoticed -- review-s4b-round2-no-spawn.mjs's own
-- finding).
--
-- This script drives the REAL production driver through REAL scripted pad input (emu.setInput on
-- inputPolled, the same technique test/lua/camera_check.lua.template already uses) -- per the
-- brief's own ruling 4: "new, separate Lua checks armed by the real driver walking the player".
--
-- Grid: 3 wide x 61 tall (build_sw_driver_timing_roms.mjs's own GRID_W/GRID_H -- 61 is the real
-- UNROM 512 PRG-region packing ceiling this slice's own ruling-N investigation established:
-- roughly one 8 KB region per grid ROW regardless of width), landing at screen(1,58) -- an interior
-- column and two rows from the tall edge, "away from any edge" (design-streamed-worlds.md ~1503).
-- The busy phase is TWO LEGS, not one: Right (crossing the column axis, landing on a screen with 8
-- real chaser actors -- MAX_ENTITIES, contact damage, the same canonical "busiest legal" shape
-- design-streamed-worlds.md ~2413 and proto-tools/diag_f18_busy_frame.mjs already use for the
-- ordinary engine's own worst mainline body), then immediately Down with NO idle gap in between
-- (crossing the row axis, landing on a screen with 4 actors -- deliberately a DIFFERENT count so its
-- own ent_active transition is distinguishable from the first) -- covering both orientations' strip
-- fetches AND a release-handoff from one held axis directly to another, in the same real walk.
--
-- Measures TWO spans per frame, both anchored on real, zero-cost engine labels (a label costs the
-- cartridge nothing -- the same precedent main_loop_after_player already set):
--   1. The DRIVER span: `update_player` entry (engine/player.asm) to `main_loop_after_player`
--      (engine/boot.asm) -- sw_update_player's own whole per-frame dispatch (movement, boundary
--      probes, hazard/encounter checks, the crossing handlers). This is what obligation 1's own
--      "driver" wording names.
--   2. The MAINLINE span: `main_loop_body_start` (engine/boot.asm, placed immediately after
--      wait_vblank returns -- fix round 2's own new label) to `main_loop_ready` (engine/boot.asm,
--      reached once update_player/update_entities/build_oam/draw_entities/draw_ui have all already
--      run, right before the NMI handshake). This is the REAL whole-frame game-logic cost the frame
--      inequality (mainline + worst NMI < 29,780-cycle NTSC frame budget) needs -- it is what
--      actually carries the 8 real chasers' own update_entities AI cost, which the driver span
--      (anchored before update_entities even runs) cannot see.
--   3. The NMI span (round-3 gate closure, Task 1): `nmi` entry (engine/boot.asm) to `nmi_rti` exit
--      (engine/boot.asm) -- the WHOLE real NMI handler, including whatever vram_drain actually had
--      queued (real crossing/flip/spawn packets, not a synthetic one), during this exact busy walk's
--      own mixed-queue traffic. vram_len is sampled at nmi entry (before the drain consumes it) so
--      every sample is a real (queue depth, cost) pair -- replaces the old hardcoded jsnes
--      WORST_NMI_CYCLES=1543 with a real measurement. Fix round 1 (R4): the per-byte extrapolation
--      this used to project to CLAUDE.md's own cited worst-case shared-frame queue depth ("flip_
--      tick, flash_tick and one frozen-world tick are the three producers that can share a frame
--      (worst case 81 of 256 bytes)") is REMOVED -- workload (e) below now triggers Shake/Flash/Sfx
--      organically inside this same scripted walk instead, so the worst mixed-queue frame this
--      script reports is a real measurement, not a projection.
--
-- Originally the driver span was anchored on `update_entities`' own entry instead -- correct only
-- under the pre-finding-1 assumption that a streamed crossing never sets screen_fresh. Finding 1
-- (fix round 1, ruling A) made a continuous ownership crossing do the real crossing work -- and set
-- screen_fresh -- from inside update_player itself, so main_loop's own screen_fresh check now
-- legitimately SKIPS update_entities on exactly that frame: the one frame this harness most needs to
-- measure. main_loop_after_player fixes this (see fix round 1's own report for the full story).
--
-- Per driver-span frame, this script reads sw_col/sw_row/st_active/ent_active both before and after
-- the span to classify the frame's own real workload:
--   * "ownership-crossing" -- sw_col OR sw_row changed this frame (a real cross_right/cross_down +
--     sw_locate_current + spawn_entities all ran).
--   * "busy-arm" -- st_active transitioned 0 -> nonzero this frame (a fresh strip armed, not merely
--     one already draining).
--   * "respawn evidence" -- on ANY crossing frame, the sum of the 8 ent_active bytes (MAX_ENTITIES)
--     must differ before vs after: the three screens on this walk carry 0/8/4 actors respectively
--     (all distinct), so a real spawn always changes the sum and a skipped one (the no-spawn negative
--     control) never does. Checked and enforced (EXIT_NO_RESPAWN_EVIDENCE, immediate) on every
--     crossing frame, not merely logged -- this is the ownership/respawn evidence ruling M asks for,
--     independent of sw_col/sw_row alone.
-- The worst (maximum) cycle span seen across BOTH classes during the BUSY phase is what this script
-- reports for the driver span (encoded in the exit code when REPORT_METRIC picks it) -- the real,
-- combined worst-path figure obligation 1 asks the frame-bound arithmetic to be recomputed from. The
-- mainline span's own busy-phase max is reported the same way when REPORT_METRIC="mainline".
--
-- Missing-workload negative control (self-contained, asserted inside this same run, driver span
-- only -- the mainline span's own missing-workload control, "0 actors vs 8/4 actors", needs a SEPARATE
-- ROM build, since actor placement is baked into the ROM at build time -- see
-- build_sw_driver_timing_roms.mjs's own --no-actors flag and run_sw_driver_timing_check.sh's own
-- comparison): the IDLE phase's own max span must be markedly cheaper than the BUSY phase's max.
--
-- Extra-cost negative control (driver span) lives OUTSIDE this template, in
-- build_sw_driver_timing_roms.mjs's own --break=unconditional-arm mode (see that file's header).
-- The no-spawn negative control (respawn evidence) also lives there, --break=no-spawn.
--
-- This is a TEMPLATE, not the script Mesen runs directly. test/lua/build_sw_driver_timing_roms.mjs
-- instantiates it, substituting the __PLACEHOLDER__ tokens below with addresses resolved fresh out
-- of that exact build's own game.fns / constants.asm / config.inc.
--
--   node test/lua/build_sw_driver_timing_roms.mjs [outDir]
--     [--break=unconditional-arm|no-spawn|slow-driver]
--     [--idle-only] [--no-actors] [--row8] [--row8-drop] [--align-landing-y] [--skip-contact]
--     [--skip-release-fallback] [--skip-shake-flash]
--   Mesen --testRunner <outDir>/sw_driver_timing.lua <outDir>/sw_driver_timing.nes
--
-- Fix round 1 (gates round-1, Task 1): five more workloads, asserted from real state observation
-- (never assumed from the walk's own shape), each with its own workload-drop sabotage flag:
--   (a) contact damage      -- player_iframes 0 -> IFRAME_TIME on a real hit (checked at
--                              main_loop_ready, since entity_contact runs inside update_entities,
--                              AFTER the driver span this template otherwise measures).
--   (b) 8 actors, row arm   -- a separate --row8 build (see run_sw_driver_timing_check.sh); this
--                              template's own EXPECT_RESPAWN_EVIDENCE is forced off for it. Fix
--                              round 2 (gates round-2 finding 1): EXPECT_ROW8 now REQUIRES a real
--                              observation -- a fresh ROW-axis arm (st_active 0->2) on the
--                              Down-target screen (sw_col/sw_row == the builder's own
--                              DOWN_TARGET_COL/ROW, not a hardcoded literal) with all eight
--                              ent_active slots occupied, inside the measured busy phase. An
--                              eight-slot observation on the Right target (which the ordinary,
--                              non---row8 build already carries) does NOT satisfy this -- it is
--                              gated on the Down-target identity and the ROW arm specifically.
--                              --row8-drop is this workload's own drop control.
--   (c) release-fallback    -- sw_axis_pref flips 0->1 on a frame with NO fresh pad_new press and
--                              pad itself already showing only the row axis held (engine/
--                              streamworld.asm:3317-3319's own fallback reassignment, distinct from
--                              the ordinary fresh-press newy path at :3301-3303).
--   (d) unaligned 3-screen  -- on the frame a column strip freshly arms, win_row_screen/win_row_local
--                              (the window's own vertical position) must show the 30-block-tall
--                              entering edge spanning three distinct screen-rows, not two, AND
--                              (round-3 gate closure Task 1, gates round-3 finding 1) the ACTUAL
--                              entering column -- sw_ss_sc, sw_stream_start_col's own saved operand
--                              (engine/streamworld.asm:1569-1571), not the window's own near-edge
--                              win_col_screen, which a positive-column arm's entering edge sits 31
--                              blocks past and can stay in-bounds even when the real entering column
--                              is off-map -- and all three entering screen-rows must be in grid
--                              bounds (< GRID_W / < GRID_H), so an off-map fill cannot satisfy it.
--                              --break=offmap-column is this workload's own off-map negative control.
--   (e) Shake/Flash/Sfx vs a live strip -- (fix round 2, gates round-2 finding 2) at a real NMI
--                              entry, vram_ready ~= 0 AND 0 < vram_len <= MIXED_VBLANK_MAX_BYTES AND
--                              st_active ~= 0 -- the exact preconditions engine/boot.asm's
--                              nmi_vram_dispatch reduced-streaming branch (~490) itself tests, not
--                              merely vram_len>0 (which a not-ready or oversized queue could also
--                              satisfy without ever taking that branch).
--
-- (test/lua/run_sw_driver_timing_check.sh runs the unbroken pass and the broken comparisons.)
